"4                                                                 Sylwia Cichacz and Karol Suchan (https://dmtcs.episciences.org/14599/pdf)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Therefore we improve the result by Cichacz and Tuza (2022) and, towards proving Conjecture 1.9, we leave open only the question if, for (ρ mod 6) = 5, Γ has not only 4-ZSPP, but also 3-ZSPP.\n4                                                                 Sylwia Cichacz and Karol Suchan\n\n**Conjecture 1.7** ((Tannenbaum, 1981)). *Let* Γ *be a finite Abelian group of order* m *with* |I(Γ)| > 1. *Let* R = Γ* \\ I(Γ). *For every positive integer* t *and integer partition* {m_i}^t_{i=1} *of* m − 1 *with* m_i ≥ 2 *for every* 1 ≤ i ≤ |R|/2 *and* m_i ≥ 3 *for every* |R|/2 + 1 ≤ i ≤ t, *there is a subset partition* {S_i}^t_{i=1} *of* Γ* *such that* |S_i| = m_i *and* ∑_{s∈S_i} s = 0 *for every* 1 ≤ i ≤ t.\n\nIt was shown independently by a few authors that the conjecture is also true for Γ ≅ (Z₂)^n with n > 1 (notice that in this case I(Γ) = Γ*, so R = ∅).\n\n**Theorem 1.8** ((Caccetta and Jia, 1997; Egawa, 1997; Tannenbaum, 1983)). *Let* Γ ≅ (Z₂)^n *with* n > 1, *then* Γ *has 3-ZSPP.*\n\nNote that, in general, even the weaker version of Conjecture 1.7 posed by Cichacz (2018) is still open.\n\n**Conjecture 1.9** ((Cichacz, 2018)). *Let* Γ *be a finite Abelian group with* |I(Γ)| > 1. *Then* Γ *has 3-ZSPP.*\n\nFor every Abelian group Γ with more than one involution and large enough order, first Cichacz and Tuza (2022) showed that it has 4-ZSPP, and later Müyesser and Pokrovskiy (2022) showed that it indeed has 3-ZSPP. This result was improved by Cichacz and Suchan (2023), for every Γ of order 2^n for some integer n > 1 such that I(Γ) ≠ 1, with the following theorem.\n\n**Theorem 1.10** ((Cichacz and Suchan, 2023)). *Let* Γ *be such that* |I(Γ)| > 1 *and* |Γ| = 2^n *for some integer* n > 1. *Then* Γ *has 3-ZSPP.*\n\nRecall that, for every Abelian group Γ, there exist groups L and H with |L| = 2^η for a nonnegative integer η and |H| = ρ for an odd positive integer ρ such that Γ ≅ L × H. Moreover, a finite Abelian group Γ has no involutions if and only if its order is odd. So, if η = 0, then Γ has 2-ZSPP by Theorem 1.6. On the other hand, if ρ = 1, then Γ has 3-ZSPP by Theorem 1.10.\n\nLet Γ be an Abelian group such that Γ ≅ L × H, where |L| = 2^η and |H| = ρ for a positive integer η and an odd positive integer ρ, with ρ > 1. The main contribution of this paper is to show that Γ has 3-ZSPP if (ρ mod 6) ∈ {1,3}. Moreover, for (ρ mod 6) = 5, we show that Γ has 4-ZSPP. Therefore we improve the result by Cichacz and Tuza (2022) and, towards proving Conjecture 1.9, we leave open only the question if, for (ρ mod 6) = 5, Γ has not only 4-ZSPP, but also 3-ZSPP. We also show that Conjecture 1.7 holds only for a group Γ with |I(Γ)| = 3 or Γ ≅ (Z₂)^n with any n > 1. As a complement, we present some applications in irregular, magic-type, and antimagic-type graph labeling to illustrate the relations of zero-sum group partitions to graph theory.\n\nIn proofs, we use zero-sum partitions of ((Z₂)^3 × Z₃)*, ((Z₂)^3 × Z₅)*, and ((Z₂)^3 × Z₇)* as base cases. They were analyzed by a computer program that we created, and sample zero-sum partitions that certify that the corresponding sequences are realizable are given in the annexes.\n\n## 2  Main Result\n\nLet Bij(Γ) denote the set of all bijections from Γ to itself. A complete mapping of a group A is defined as φ ∈ Bij(A) such that the mapping θ: g ↦ g^{-1}φ(g) is also bijective (some authors refer to θ, rather than φ, as the complete mapping) (Mann, 1944). In the proof of our main result we will use the following property of complete mappings:\n\n**Lemma 2.1** ((Cichacz, 2018; Zeng, 2015)). *Let* Γ *be a finite Abelian group such that* |I(Γ)| ≠ 1. *Then there exist* φ, ϕ ∈ Bij(Γ) *(not necessarily distinct) such that* g + φ(g) + ϕ(g) = 0 *for every* g ∈ Γ. *In particular, we may assume that* φ(0) = ϕ(0) = 0.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProblems, proofs, and disproofs on the inversion number | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2212.09188v3)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Crawled: last week; @misc{aubian2022problemsproofsdisproofsinversion, title={Problems, proofs, and disproofs on the inversion number}, author={Guillaume Aubian and Frédéric Havet and Florian Hörsch and Felix Klingelhoefer and Nicolas Nisse and Clément Rambaud and Quentin Vermande}, year={2022}, eprint={2212.09188}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09188}, }\nSubmitted 23 Dec 2022\n\n# Problems, proofs, and disproofs on the inversion number\n\nGA\n\nGuillaume Aubian\n\nFH\n\nFrédéric Havet\n\nFH\n\nFlorian Hörsch\n\nFK\n\nFelix KlingelhoeferNicolas Nisse\n\nCR\n\nClément Rambaud\n\nQV\n\nQuentin Vermande\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe inversion of a set $X$X of vertices in a digraph $D$D consists in reversing the direction of all arcs of $D\\langle X\\rangle$D⟨X⟩. The inversion number of an oriented graph $D$D, denoted by ${\\rm inv}(D)$inv(D), is the minimum number of inversions needed to transform $D$D into an acyclic oriented graph. In this paper, we study a number of problems involving the inversion number of oriented graphs. Firstly, we give bounds on ${\\rm inv}(n)$inv(n), the maximum of the inversion numbers of the oriented graphs of order $n$n. We show $n - \\mathcal{O}(\\sqrt{n\\log n}) \\ \\leq \\ {\\rm inv}(n) \\ \\leq \\ n - \\lceil \\log (n+1) \\rceil$n−O(n lo g n​) ≤ inv(n) ≤ n−⌈lo g(n+1)⌉. Secondly, we disprove a conjecture of Bang-Jensen et al. asserting that, for every pair of oriented graphs $L$L and $R$R, we have ${\\rm inv}(L\\Rightarrow R) ={\\rm inv}(L) + {\\rm inv}(R)$inv(L⇒R)=inv(L)+inv(R), where $L\\Rightarrow R$L⇒R is the oriented graph obtained from the disjoint union of $L$L and $R$R by adding all arcs from $L$L to $R$R. Finally, we investigate whether, for all pairs of positive integers $k_1,k_2$k 1​,k 2​, there exists an integer $f(k_1,k_2)$f(k 1​,k 2​) such that if $D$D is an oriented graph with ${\\rm inv}(D) \\geq f(k_1,k_2)$inv(D)≥f(k 1​,k 2​) then there is a partition $(V_1, V_2)$(V 1​,V 2​) of $V(D)$V(D) such that ${\\rm inv}(D\\langle V_i\\rangle) \\geq k_i$inv(D⟨V i​⟩)≥k i​ for $i=1,2$i=1,2. We show that $f(1,k)$f(1,k) exists and $f(1,k)\\leq k+10$f(1,k)≤k+10 for all positive integers $k$k. Further, we show that $f(k_1,k_2)$f(k 1​,k 2​) exists for all pairs of positive integers $k_1,k_2$k 1​,k 2​ when the oriented graphs in consideration are restricted to be tournaments.\n\n@misc{aubian2022problemsproofsdisproofsinversion, title={Problems, proofs, and disproofs on the inversion number}, author={Guillaume Aubian and Frédéric Havet and Florian Hörsch and Felix Klingelhoefer and Nicolas Nisse and Clément Rambaud and Quentin Vermande}, year={2022}, eprint={2212.09188}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09188}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNon-partition-regularity of the four-term pattern over bounded-degree polynomial semirings | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/356718/non-partition-regularity-of-the-four-term-pattern-over-bound)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Bounded-degree obstruction conjecture. ... The conjecture is known for $d=1$d=1, using the two-coloring that colors reducible polynomials red and irreducible polynomials blue; whether the pattern is non-partition-regular over every finite-degree $P_d$P d​ remains open. ... Ryan Alweiss, “Monochromatic Sums and Products of Polynomials”, arXiv:2211.00766 (2024).\n\n# Non-partition-regularity of the four-term pattern over bounded-degree polynomial semirings\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nFor $d\\geq 1$d≥1, let $P_d$P d​ be the set of polynomials in countably many variables with nonnegative integer coefficients, constant term, and degree at most $d$d in each variable. A finite pattern is partition regular over $P_d$P d​ if every finite coloring of $P_d$P d​ contains a monochromatic realization of the pattern. Bounded-degree obstruction conjecture. If $1\\leq d<\\infty$1≤d<∞, then\n\n$$\\{x,y,x+y,xy\\}$${x,y,x+y,x y}\n\nis not partition regular over $P_d$P d​. The conjecture is known for $d=1$d=1, using the two-coloring that colors reducible polynomials red and irreducible polynomials blue; whether the pattern is non-partition-regular over every finite-degree $P_d$P d​ remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nRyan Alweiss, “Monochromatic Sums and Products of Polynomials”, arXiv:2211.00766 (2024).\n\npartition-regularitypolynomial-ringsramsey-theory\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . (https://dmtcs.episciences.org/10998/pdf)\nciteturn6search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; Theorem 1.6 (Caccetta and Jia (1997); Egawa (1997); Tannenbaum (1983)). ... Note that, in general, even the weaker version of Conjecture 1.5 posed by Cichacz (2018) is still open. ... der 2n, for a positive integer n, such that |I(Γ)| > 1 has 3-ZSPP.\nΓ has 2-ZSPP if and only if\n|I(Γ)| ∈{0, 3}.\nThe above theorem conﬁrms, for the case of |I(Γ)| = 3, the following conjecture stated by Tannen-\nbaum.\ns∈Si s = 0 for every i ∈[1, t].\nConjecture 1.5 (Tannenbaum (1981)). Let Γ be a ﬁnite Abelian group of order m with |I(Γ)| > 1. Let\nR = Γ \\ (I(Γ) ∪{0}). For every positive integer t and every integer partition {mi}t\ni=1 of m −1, with\nmi ≥2 for every i ∈[1, |R|/2], and mi ≥3 for every i ∈[|R|/2 + 1, t], there is a subset partition\n{Si}t\ni=1 of Γ∗such that |Si| = mi and P\nIt was shown independently by a few authors that Conjecture 1.5 is also true for Γ ∼= (Z2)n for any\ninteger n, with n > 1 (Note that in this case I(Γ) = Γ∗, so R = ∅).\nTheorem 1.6 (Caccetta and Jia (1997); Egawa (1997); Tannenbaum (1983)). Let Γ ∼= (Z2)n for some\ninteger n, with n > 1, then Γ has 3-ZSPP.\nNote that, in general, even the weaker version of Conjecture 1.5 posed by Cichacz (2018) is still open.\nConjecture 1.7 (Cichacz (2018)). Let Γ be a ﬁnite Abelian group with |I(Γ)| > 1. Then Γ has 3-ZSPP.\nRecently Cichacz and Tuza (2022) proved that, if |Γ| is large enough and |I(Γ)| > 1, then Γ has\n4-ZSPP.\nThe main contribution of this paper is to show that Conjecture 1.7 holds for every Abelian group Γ\nthe cardinality of which is a power of 2 and |I(Γ)| > 1. This result is a generalization of the result\nfor Γ ∼= (Z2)n by Caccetta and Jia (1997); Egawa (1997); Tannenbaum (1983). The structure of the\npaper is as follows. In Section 2, we start by establishing the result for Γ ∼= Z4 × (Z2)n−2, for any\ninteger n with n ≥3. Then, in Section 3, we prove the main result that any Abelian group Γ of or-\nder 2n, for a positive integer n, such that |I(Γ)| > 1 has 3-ZSPP. We use some methods developed\nby Egawa (1997); Zeng (2015). Subset partitions of\n\u0000\nZ4 × (Z2)2\u0001∗,\n\u0000\nZ4 × (Z2)3\u0001∗,\n\u0000\nZ4 × (Z2)4\u0001∗,\n\u0000\nZ4 × (Z2)5\u0001∗,\n\u0000\n(Z4)2 × Z2\n\u0001∗,\n\u0000\n(Z4)3\u0001∗,\n\u0000\nZ8 × (Z2)2\u0001∗, and\n\u0000\nZ4 × (Z2)2\u0001∗+\n\u0000\n(Z2)2\u0001∗were analyzed\nby a computer program that we created, and sample zero-sum partitions that certify that the corresponding\nsequences are realizable are given in the annexes. In Section 4, we present some applications of our main\nresult. In Section 5, we conclude with some ﬁnal remarks and propose some conjectures.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe inversion number of dijoins and blow-up digraphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2404.14937v2)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; The inversion number of $D$, denoted by $inv(D)$, is the minimum number of inversions needed to transform $D$ into an acyclic digraph. ... Thus we disprove the conjecture of Aubian el at. \\cite{2212.09188} and the conjecture of Alon el at. \\cite{2212.11969}.\n\n## The inversion number of dijoins and blow-up digraphs\n\nHaozhe Wang, Yuxuan Yang, Mei Lu\n\nPublished: 2024-04-23 | Updated: 2024-04-25\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\ngraph tournament\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nFor an oriented graph $D$, the $inversion$ of $X \\subseteq V(D)$ in $D$ is the digraph obtained from $D$ by reversing the direction of all arcs with both ends in $X$. The inversion number of $D$, denoted by $inv(D)$, is the minimum number of inversions needed to transform $D$ into an acyclic digraph. In this paper, we first show that $inv (\\overrightarrow{C_3} \\Rightarrow D)= inv(D) +1$ for any oriented graph $\\textit{D}$ with even inversion number $inv(D)$, where the dijoin $\\overrightarrow{C_3} \\Rightarrow D$ is the oriented graph obtained from the disjoint union of $\\overrightarrow{C_3}$ and $D$ by adding all arcs from $\\overrightarrow{C_3}$ to $D$. Thus we disprove the conjecture of Aubian el at. \\cite{2212.09188} and the conjecture of Alon el at. \\cite{2212.11969}. We also study the blow-up graph which is an oriented graph obtained from a tournament by replacing all vertices into oriented graphs. We construct a tournament $T$ with order $n$ and $inv(T)=\\frac{n}{3}+1$ using blow-up graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe inversion number of dijoins and blow-up digraphs - Science Explorer Abstract (https://scixplorer.org/abs/2024arXiv240414937W/abstract)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The inversion number of $D$, denoted by $inv(D)$, is the minimum number of inversions needed to transform $D$ into an acyclic digraph. ... Thus we disprove the conjecture of Aubian el at. \\cite{2212.09188} and the conjecture of Alon el at. \\cite{2212.11969}.\n\n## The inversion number of dijoins and blow-up digraphs\n\n### Authors\n\nWang, Haozhe;\n\nYang, Yuxuan;\n\nLu, Mei\n\nEprint\n\nFull Text Sources\n\nOther Resources\n\n### Abstract\n\nFor an oriented graph $D$, the $inversion$ of $X \\subseteq V(D)$ in $D$ is the digraph obtained from $D$ by reversing the direction of all arcs with both ends in $X$. The inversion number of $D$, denoted by $inv(D)$, is the minimum number of inversions needed to transform $D$ into an acyclic digraph. In this paper, we first show that $inv (\\overrightarrow{C_3} \\Rightarrow D)= inv(D) +1$ for any oriented graph $\\textit{D}$ with even inversion number $inv(D)$, where the dijoin $\\overrightarrow{C_3} \\Rightarrow D$ is the oriented graph obtained from the disjoint union of $\\overrightarrow{C_3}$ and $D$ by adding all arcs from $\\overrightarrow{C_3}$ to $D$. Thus we disprove the conjecture of Aubian el at. \\cite{2212.09188} and the conjecture of Alon el at. \\cite{2212.11969}. We also study the blow-up graph which is an oriented graph obtained from a tournament by replacing all vertices into oriented graphs. We construct a tournament $T$ with order $n$ and $inv(T)=\\frac{n}{3}+1$ using blow-up graphs.\n\n### Details\n\nPublication  | eprint arXiv:2404.14937\nPublication Date  | April 2024\nDOI  | 10.48550/arXiv.2404.14937\narXiv  | arXiv:2404.14937\nBibcode  | 2024arXiv240414937W\nCollection  | general\nKeyword  | Mathematics - Combinatorics--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSharp $\\ell^p$ inequalities for discrete singular integrals on the lattice $\\mathbb{Z}^d$ | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/zh/abs/2209.09737)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This paper investigates higher dimensional versions of the longstanding conjecture verified in [Ba\\~nuelos and Kwa\\'snicki, Duke Math. ... @misc{bañuelos2024sharpellpinequalitiesdiscrete, title={Sharp $\\ell^p$ inequalities for discrete singular integrals on the lattice $\\mathbb{Z}^d$}, author={Rodrigo Bañuelos and Daesung Kim and Mateusz Kwaśnicki}, year={2024}, eprint={2209.09737}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.PR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.09737}, }\nSubmitted 2024年9月2日\n\n# Sharp $\\ell^p$ℓ p inequalities for discrete singular integrals on the lattice $\\mathbb{Z}^d$Z d\n\nImagePurdue UniversityImageGeorgia Institute of Technology Wrocław University of Science and Technology\n\nRB\n\nRodrigo Bañuelos\n\nDK\n\nDaesung Kim\n\nMK\n\nMateusz Kwaśnicki\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis paper investigates higher dimensional versions of the longstanding conjecture verified in [Ba\\~nuelos and Kwa\\'snicki, Duke Math. J. (2019)] that the $\\ell^p$ℓ p-norm of the discrete Hilbert transform on the integers is the same as the $L^p$L p-norm of the Hilbert transform on the real line. It computes the $\\ell^p$ℓ p-norms of a family of discrete operators on the lattice $\\mathbb{Z}^{d}$Z d, $d\\geq 1.$d≥1. They are discretizations of a new class of singular integrals on $\\mathbb{R}^d$R d that have the same kernels as the classical Riesz transforms near zero and similar behavior at infinity. The discrete operators have the same $p$p-norms as the classical Riesz transforms on $\\mathbb{R}^d$R d. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe inversion number of the tournament Q_n | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/381872/the-inversion-number-of-the-tournament-q-n)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Guillaume Aubian, Frédéric Havet, Florian Hörsch, Felix Klingelhoefer, Nicolas Nisse, Clément Rambaud and Quentin Vermande, “Problems, proofs, and disproofs on the inversion number”, arXiv:2212.09188 (2022).\n\n# The inversion number of the tournament $Q_n$Q n​\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nLet $Q_n$Q n​ be the tournament obtained from the transitive tournament of order $n$n by reversing the arcs of its unique directed Hamiltonian path.\n\nThe $Q_n$Q n​ inversion-number conjecture.\n\n$$\\operatorname{inv}(Q_n)=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor.$$inv(Q n​)=⌊2 n−1​⌋.\n\nThe source introduces this as the explicit-tournament motivation for Belkhechine et al.'s lower-bound conjecture. The source does not state a resolution.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nGuillaume Aubian, Frédéric Havet, Florian Hörsch, Felix Klingelhoefer, Nicolas Nisse, Clément Rambaud and Quentin Vermande, “Problems, proofs, and disproofs on the inversion number”, arXiv:2212.09188 (2022).\n\noriented-graphstournaments\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCichacz's 4-ZSPP conjecture for Abelian groups with more than one involution | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/365571/cichacz-s-4-zspp-conjecture-for-abelian-groups-with-more-tha)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Cichacz's 4-ZSPP conjecture for Abelian groups with more than one involution ... If $|I(\\Gamma)|>1$∣I(Γ)∣>1, then $\\Gamma$Γ has $3$3-ZSPP.\n\n# Cichacz's 4-ZSPP conjecture for Abelian groups with more than one involution\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nLet $\\Gamma$Γ be a finite Abelian group, and let $I(\\Gamma)$I(Γ) denote its set of involutions. A finite Abelian group has 4-ZSPP if every integer partition of $|\\Gamma|-1$∣Γ∣−1 whose parts are all at least $4$4 can be realized by a partition of $\\Gamma^*=\\Gamma\\setminus\\{0\\}$Γ∗=Γ∖{0} into zero-sum subsets of the corresponding sizes. Cichacz's 4-ZSPP conjecture. If $|I(\\Gamma)|>1$∣I(Γ)∣>1, then $\\Gamma$Γ has $3$3-ZSPP. The surrounding text records later results proving the stronger assertion that every such group has $4$4-ZSPP, so the stated conjecture is solved.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nSylwia Cichacz, “Disjoint zero-sum subsets in Abelian groups and its application – survey”, arXiv:2410.22245 (2024).\n\n## Additional references\n\n2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2022–2024). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2203.09395.\n\nadditive-combinatoricsfinite-abelian-groupszero-sum-problems\n\nPosted 2 months ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrict hierarchy conjecture for polynomial partition regularity | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/356719/strict-hierarchy-conjecture-for-polynomial-partition-regular)\nciteturn6search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; This proposes that partition regularity over the bounded-degree polynomial settings has genuinely distinct levels; the paper gives the conjecture as complementary to the non-partition-regularity conjecture for $\\{x,y,x+y,xy\\}${x,y,x+y,x y}, and no general resolution is supplied. ... Ryan Alweiss, “Monochromatic Sums and Products of Polynomials”, arXiv:2211.00766 (2024).\n\n# Strict hierarchy conjecture for polynomial partition regularity\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nFor $d\\geq 1$d≥1, let $P_d$P d​ be the set of polynomials in countably many variables with nonnegative integer coefficients, constant term, and degree at most $d$d in each variable. A pattern is partition regular over $P_d$P d​ if every finite coloring of $P_d$P d​ contains a monochromatic realization of the pattern, and it appears in $P_i$P i​ if its one-coloring contains a monochromatic copy of $P_i$P i​. Strict hierarchy conjecture. For all $1\\leq i<j$1≤i<j, there exists a pattern $\\mathcal{F}$F such that $\\mathcal{F}$F is partition regular in $P_j$P j​ but not in $P_i$P i​, while $\\mathcal{F}$F appears in $P_i$P i​. This proposes that partition regularity over the bounded-degree polynomial settings has genuinely distinct levels; the paper gives the conjecture as complementary to the non-partition-regularity conjecture for $\\{x,y,x+y,xy\\}${x,y,x+y,x y}, and no general resolution is supplied.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nRyan Alweiss, “Monochromatic Sums and Products of Polynomials”, arXiv:2211.00766 (2024).\n\npartition-regularitypolynomial-ringsramsey-theory\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe inversion number of dijoins and blow-up digraphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14937)\nciteturn6academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; The inversion number of $D$, denoted by $inv(D)$, is the minimum number of inversions needed to transform $D$ into an acyclic digraph. ... Thus we disprove the conjecture of Aubian el at. \\cite{2212.09188} and the conjecture of Alon el at. \\cite{2212.11969}.\nTitle: The inversion number of dijoins and blow-up digraphs\nAuthors: Haozhe Wang, Yuxuan Yang, Mei Lu\nDate: Tue Apr 23 11:25:33 2024\n\nFor an oriented graph $D$, the $inversion$ of $X \\subseteq V(D)$ in $D$ is the digraph obtained from $D$ by reversing the direction of all arcs with both ends in $X$. The inversion number of $D$, denoted by $inv(D)$, is the minimum number of inversions needed to transform $D$ into an acyclic digraph. In this paper, we first show that $inv (\\overrightarrow{C_3} \\Rightarrow D)= inv(D) +1$ for any oriented graph $\\textit{D}$ with even inversion number $inv(D)$, where the dijoin $\\overrightarrow{C_3} \\Rightarrow D$ is the oriented graph obtained from the disjoint union of $\\overrightarrow{C_3}$ and $D$ by adding all arcs from $\\overrightarrow{C_3}$ to $D$. Thus we disprove the conjecture of Aubian el at. \\cite{2212.09188} and the conjecture of Alon el at. \\cite{2212.11969}. We also study the blow-up graph which is an oriented graph obtained from a tournament by replacing all vertices into oriented graphs. We construct a tournament $T$ with order $n$ and $inv(T)=\\frac{n}{3}+1$ using blow-up graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArxiver (https://arxiver.lazybrains.com/author/1516541)\nciteturn6search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Deciding if a tournament is 2-colorable is NP-hard. ... In this paper, we study a number of problems involving the inversion number of oriented graphs. ... arXiv:2212.09188\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSharp $\\ell^p$ inequalities for discrete singular integrals | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/363697538_Sharp_ellp_inequalities_for_discrete_singular_integrals)\nciteturn6search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2209.09737 ... The construction for $d>1$ is motivated by a similar problem, Conjecture 5.5, concerning the norm of the discrete Riesz transforms arising from discretizing singular integrals on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ as in the original paper of A.\n---------